.Dd August 11, 2026
.Dt PLAKAR-KINDLE 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm plakar-kindle
.Nd Kindle integration for plakar
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm kindle
integration backs up the contents of a Kindle e-reader attached over USB into a
Kloset repository, and restores them onto a device.
.Pp
It covers what Amazon's cloud sync does not: side-loaded books, the annotations
attached to them, and
.Pa My Clippings.txt ,
all of which exist only on the device itself.
.Sh CONFIGURATION
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm location
Required.
.Pa kindle://
to auto-detect the attached device, or
.Pa kindle:///path/to/mountpoint
to name it explicitly.
An explicit path is needed when several Kindles are attached, when the device is
mounted over MTP or FUSE, or when working from a copy of a Kindle tree on disk.
.El
.Pp
Importer only:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm documents
Back up the books.
Defaults to
.Dv true .
.It Cm annotations
Back up the
.Pa .sdr
sidecar directories, holding highlights, bookmarks and reading positions, plus
.Pa My Clippings.txt .
Defaults to
.Dv true .
.It Cm system
Back up the device's
.Pa system
directory: collections and settings.
Defaults to
.Dv false ,
as these describe one specific device.
.It Cm everything
Back up the whole volume verbatim, caches included, ignoring the options above.
Defaults to
.Dv false .
.El
.Pp
Exporter only:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm system
Allow restoring into the device's
.Pa system
directory.
Defaults to
.Dv false ,
because those files describe one particular device and overwriting them with
another Kindle's settings can confuse the firmware.
.El
.Pp
Thumbnails and search indexes are always excluded unless
.Cm everything
is set: they are large, the device rebuilds them on demand, and they are
meaningless on a different one.
.Sh CONNECTING THE DEVICE
Kindles up to the Paperwhite 3, and the Basic through its 10th generation, mount
as a removable volume and are found automatically.
.Pp
The Paperwhite 4 onwards, the Oasis, the Scribe and the Colorsoft use MTP, which
is not a filesystem and must be mounted first.
On Linux, gvfs mounts such devices under
.Ev XDG_RUNTIME_DIR Pa /gvfs ,
which is searched automatically;
.Xr jmtpfs 1
and
.Xr simple-mtpfs 1
are alternatives.
macOS has no MTP support, so a third-party client is required.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Back up the attached Kindle:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ plakar source add mykindle kindle://
$ plakar at /var/backups/kindle backup @mykindle
.Ed
.Pp
Back up books without annotations:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ plakar source add books kindle:// annotations=false
.Ed
.Pp
Restore onto a device:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ plakar destination add newkindle kindle://
$ plakar at /var/backups/kindle restore -to @newkindle <snapid>
.Ed
.Sh NOTES
Restores are written through a temporary file and renamed into place, so a
Kindle unplugged mid-transfer keeps the previous copy of a book rather than a
truncated one.
.Pp
Ownership and permissions are normalised rather than preserved: a Kindle's
filesystem carries neither, and what the operating system synthesises depends on
the mount options of whoever attached it.
.Pp
Books bought from Amazon are DRM-protected and tied to the purchasing account.
They are backed up and restored faithfully, but a restored copy is readable only
on a device registered to that account.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr plakar 1 ,
.Xr plakar-backup 1 ,
.Xr plakar-restore 1
